{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/212702-pack-totem-house-rules","id":212702,"title":"Pack Totem House Rules?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-07-29T13:09:20-07:00","id":212702,"title":"Pack Totem House Rules?","content":"I've run a few werewolf games and run into a minor issue with pack totems. Because they can be killed (or even just abandon the pack in some cases) and start imposing hefty restrictions with high points, most of my players refuse to contribute to getting a decent totem. The result is a totem with 1 to 5 points, that the players all but ignore for the rest of the campaign. Are there any common house rules for totems that most people use? Am I missing something about their mechanics that I should have seen?<br /> In my last game I designed 4 local totems that I thought the players would like, including attributes, numina, and the general ban that the totem would impose. In exchange, I gave 5 points of free bonuses, again assigned by me. I felt like this made the totem a little bit stronger (without any threat of being abusive) and have a lot more personality. I felt like the players treated the totem they went with like an honored (or at least respected) NPC for the duration of the campaign.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3512-magus2000","name":"Magus2000","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-29T13:24:17-07:00","id":212718,"title":"","content":"Also how aware of the pack members is the totem spirit supposed to be? If they always know if a ban has been broken do they know the state of all pack members at all times? Is it more of an empathic bond, so they wouldn't know if a ban is broken if the pack member(s) don't? Could they use this sense to track down missing pack members?<br /> Like if a pack member gets kidnapped and is unconscious, (thus can't enter the shadow for an hour each day, or meditate at dawn, etc.) would the totem spirit know its ban has been broken? Could it sense the direction that its ban is being broken in?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3512-magus2000","name":"Magus2000","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-08-04T13:03:18-07:00","id":218437,"title":"","content":"I ran into a similar situation with my players when the time came to choosing a Totem. The best thing, in my eyes, is to assure your players that even if they spend up to the point where a severe ban will be imposed, you're going to work with them to devise something that will make the game more fun, not less. The ban may be something that's horribly burdensome to the characters, but it should only flavor the game and make it more interesting from the perspective of the players. Perhaps you can even offer beats (if you're using GMC-style rules) when a character is significantly hampered by their ban.<br /> <br /> As for the worry of investment, I'm inclined to pull another page from the GMC: sanctity of merits. If circumstances arrange themselves so that the totem dies or leaves, you are refunded your invested merit dots and may reassign them when and as the Stortyeller deems appropriate.<br /> <br /> ----------------------<br /> <br /> On to part two!<br /> <br /> The totem bond is something that, as you point out, isn't really pinned down as far as I've read. There are merits to running violations of the pack ban with an empathetic bond or no. I tend to run it as empathetic, such that the Totem knows when its ban has been violated, but not the specifics unless it is present. I like to avoid setting up &quot;gamey&quot; situations like the one you describe, so I wouldn't allow &quot;tracking by violation ping&quot; at my table. The totem bond is supposed to satisfy a longing in an Uratha's heart, something they crave as instinctually as a pack. If the players get fixated on messing around with mechanics of it, I feel it puts emotional distance between them and the subject matter. That said, bringing the Totem along on the search sounds like it merits a dice bonus to Investigate for signs of their comrade (I'd probably make it +2.)<br /> <br /> Part of the benefit of allowing unwilling violations is what you describe with the unconscious werewolf. For one, it makes situations where the pack has been split even more dire! Pack is above family, friends, and everyone else in the world as far as the Uratha are concerned, so having a mechanic in play that will incline players to scurry and fret when the situation arises will help reinforce the importance of pack. Further, it gives the players a sneaky tool for dealing with other rival packs. Neighbor have a ban that forbids them from eating pork? Have a wolf-blooded ally slip some swine blood into their drinks and watch them scramble as they try to figure out why their totem is demanding answers for their behavior.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2320-tilting-clock","name":"Tilting Clock","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}